Hold $x$ constant. The numerator contains a composite function, so use the chain rule to differentiate it:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}e^{xy^2}
&=e^{xy^2}\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy^2)=2xy e^{xy^2}, \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+y^2)&=2y.
\end{aligned}
$$

Both numerator and denominator depend on $y$. Apply the quotient rule, preserving the subtraction and the squared denominator:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
&=\frac{(2xy e^{xy^2})(x+y^2)-e^{xy^2}(2y)}{(x+y^2)^2} \\
&=\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The factor $2y e^{xy^2}$ is common to the numerator terms; it cannot be canceled with the sum $x+y^2$ in the denominator. The derivative is defined wherever $x+y^2\ne0$.

Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=\left(x + z\right)\cos y+\cos{\left(x \right)}\cdot 1\\&=\left(x + z\right) \cos{\left(y \right)} + \cos{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $x$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=- 3 \sin{\left(z \right)}\cdot 1+\left(- 2 y - 2 z\right)\left(-\sin x\right)\\&=\left(2 y + 2 z\right) \sin{\left(x \right)} - 3 \sin{\left(z \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $x$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\left(3 y + 3 z\right)\cos x+2 \cos{\left(y \right)}\cdot 1\\&=\left(3 y + 3 z\right) \cos{\left(x \right)} + 2 \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=\left(- 3 x - 3 z\right)\cos y+- 2 \cos{\left(z \right)}\cdot 1\\&=\left(- 3 x - 3 z\right) \cos{\left(y \right)} - 2 \cos{\left(z \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=2 \sin{\left(z \right)}\cdot 1+\left(- 2 x - 2 z\right)\left(-\sin y\right)\\&=\left(2 x + 2 z\right) \sin{\left(y \right)} + 2 \sin{\left(z \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=y,\quad v=\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=1\\
v_y&=\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=1\left[\left(1\right)\left(\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\right)+\left(y\right)\left(\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=\frac{y^{2}}{\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} + \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=2$ and $y=\sqrt{5}$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(2,\sqrt{5})&=\left(1\right)\left(3\right)+\left(\sqrt{5}\right)\left(\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}\right)\\
&=\frac{14}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=x,\quad v=\tan{\left(x y^{2} \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=1\\
v_x&=y^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x y^{2} \right)}\\
r_x&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=1\left[\left(1\right)\left(\tan{\left(x y^{2} \right)}\right)+\left(x\right)\left(y^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x y^{2} \right)}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=x y^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x y^{2} \right)} + \tan{\left(x y^{2} \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=\frac{\pi}{16}$ and $y=-2$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(\frac{\pi}{16},-2)&=\left(1\right)\left(1\right)+\left(\frac{\pi}{16}\right)\left(8\right)\\
&=1 + \frac{\pi}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=y^{2},\quad v=\tan{\left(x^{2} y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=2 y\\
v_y&=x^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x^{2} y \right)}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=1\left[\left(2 y\right)\left(\tan{\left(x^{2} y \right)}\right)+\left(y^{2}\right)\left(x^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x^{2} y \right)}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=x^{2} y^{2} \sec^{2}{\left(x^{2} y \right)} + 2 y \tan{\left(x^{2} y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=-1$ and $y=- \frac{\pi}{4}$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y(-1,- \frac{\pi}{4})&=\left(- \frac{\pi}{2}\right)\left(-1\right)+\left(\frac{\pi^{2}}{16}\right)\left(2\right)\\
&=\frac{\pi^{2}}{8} + \frac{\pi}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=\cos{\left(y \right)},\quad r=0\\
u&=x^{2},\quad v=e^{x}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=2 x\\
v_x&=e^{x}\\
r_x&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=\cos{\left(y \right)}\left[\left(2 x\right)\left(e^{x}\right)+\left(x^{2}\right)\left(e^{x}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=\left(x^{2} e^{x} + 2 x e^{x}\right) \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=2$ and $y=0$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(2,0)&=\left(4\right)\left(e^{2}\right)+\left(4\right)\left(e^{2}\right)\\
&=8 e^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=y,\quad r=- \sin{\left(x \right)}\\
u&=x,\quad v=\cos{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_x&=1\\
v_x&=- \sin{\left(x \right)}\\
r_x&=- \cos{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x&=C(u_xv+uv_x)+r_x\\
&=y\left[\left(1\right)\left(\cos{\left(x \right)}\right)+\left(x\right)\left(- \sin{\left(x \right)}\right)\right]+\left(- \cos{\left(x \right)}\right)\\
&=y \left(- x \sin{\left(x \right)} + \cos{\left(x \right)}\right) - \cos{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $x=- \pi$ and $y=2$ and combine the exact values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(- \pi,2)&=2\left[\left(1\right)\left(-1\right)+\left(- \pi\right)\left(0\right)\right]+\left(1\right)\\
&=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

A square matrix is singular if and only if its determinant is zero. A singular matrix has no multiplicative inverse; a nonsingular square matrix has one.

The matching first two rows do not determine the determinant by themselves. Expand each determinant along the top row:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(P) &= 1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
-2\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
+1\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 3 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= (4-9)-2(8-9)+(6-3) \\
&= -5+2+3 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
$$

The changed entry appears in the first two minors for $Q$, so those minors must be evaluated again:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(Q) &= 1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{vmatrix}
-2\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{vmatrix}
+1\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 3 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= (5-9)-2(10-9)+(6-3) \\
&= -4-2+3 \\
&= -3.
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $P$ is singular and has no multiplicative inverse. Since $\det(Q)=-3\ne0$, $Q$ is not singular and has a multiplicative inverse. A negative determinant is still nonzero, and $\det(Q)$ is $-3$, not $3$.

Select “$P$ is a singular matrix” and “$Q$ has a multiplicative inverse, but $P$ does not.”

A square matrix is singular if and only if its determinant is zero.

Expand along the top row, using the signs $+,-,+$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(A) &= 0\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
-1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 2 & 4\end{vmatrix}
+2\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\ 2 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= 0-1(4-6)+2(3-4) \\
&= 2-2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, $A$ is singular. Select $\det(A)=0$ and “$A$ is a singular matrix.” Do not select “$A$ is not a singular matrix.”

