Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=\ln{\left(\left(y - 2\right)^{2} + \left(x - 1\right)^{2} + \left(z - 2\right)^{2} \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 \left(x - 1\right)}{\left(y - 2\right)^{2} + \left(x - 1\right)^{2} + \left(z - 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 \left(y - 2\right)}{\left(y - 2\right)^{2} + \left(x - 1\right)^{2} + \left(z - 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 \left(z - 2\right)}{\left(y - 2\right)^{2} + \left(x - 1\right)^{2} + \left(z - 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(y - 2\right)&=0\\
\left(x - 1\right)&=0\\
\left(z - 2\right)&=e^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(0\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(0\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(1,2,e^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2)=\langle 0,0,\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}}\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=\frac{1}{2}\langle 0,0,\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}}\rangle=\langle 0,0,e^{- \frac{1}{2}}\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 1,2,e^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2\rangle+t\,\langle 0,0,e^{- \frac{1}{2}}\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

Equating coordinates gives

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}x&=1\\y&=2\\z&=\frac{t}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2\end{aligned}\right..
$$

