Both projections use the same dot product, but their denominators differ:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}&=1(2)+2(-3)+2(6)=8,\\
\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert&=\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}=3,\\
\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert&=\sqrt{2^2+(-3)^2+6^2}=7.
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{b}}\mathbf{a}&=\frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert}=\frac{8}{7},\\
\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{b}&=\frac{\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{a}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert}=\frac{8}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Subtract in the requested order:

$$
\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{b}}\mathbf{a}-\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{b}
=\frac{8}{7}-\frac{8}{3}
=\frac{24-56}{21}
=-\frac{32}{21}.
$$

The dot product is symmetric; the scalar projection generally is not.

The vector we project onto is $\mathbf{b}$, so its magnitude is the denominator:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{b}}\mathbf{a}
&=\frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert}\\
&=\frac{2(1)+(-1)(2)+1(2)}{\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}}\\
&=\frac{2-2+2}{\sqrt{9}}\\
&=\frac{2}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

First use the given projection to determine the missing component. Since

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert&=\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2+2^2}=3,\\
\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}&=2t+2(-1)+(-1)(2)=2t-4.
\end{aligned}
$$

we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{2t-4}{3}&=-2,\\
2t-4&=-6,\\
t&=-1.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $\mathbf{a}=\langle-1,2,-1\rangle$. The reverse projection uses the magnitude of $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert&=\sqrt{(-1)^2+2^2+(-1)^2}=\sqrt{6},\\
\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{a}&=2(-1)+(-1)(2)+2(-1)=-6,\\
\operatorname{comp}_{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{b}
&=\frac{\mathbf{b}\cdot\mathbf{a}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert}
=\frac{-6}{\sqrt{6}}
=-\sqrt{6}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Reversing the projection keeps the dot product but changes the denominator, so the answer need not equal the given $-2$.

