The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(0)&=\left\langle 0,1\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r(1)&=\left\langle 3,2\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(0,1)&=- 2 \left(0\right)^{2} - 2 \left(1\right) \\ &=-2, \\ f(3,2)&=- 2 \left(3\right)^{2} - 2 \left(2\right) \\ &=-22.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(3,2)-f(0,1) \\ &=\left(-22\right)-\left(-2\right) \\ &=-20.
\end{aligned}
$$

The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(0)&=\left\langle 0,0\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r(-1)&=\left\langle -1,-3\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(0,0)&=2 \left(0\right) e^{2 \left(0\right)} \\ &=0, \\ f(-1,-3)&=2 \left(-3\right) e^{2 \left(-1\right)} \\ &=- \frac{6}{e^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(-1,-3)-f(0,0) \\ &=\left(- \frac{6}{e^{2}}\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=- \frac{6}{e^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(2 \pi)&=\left\langle 2 \pi,0\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r(\pi)&=\left\langle \pi,2\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(2 \pi,0)&=2 \left(2 \pi\right)^{2} \left(0\right) - 2 \left(2 \pi\right) \left(0\right)^{2} \\ &=0, \\ f(\pi,2)&=2 \left(\pi\right)^{2} \left(2\right) - 2 \left(\pi\right) \left(2\right)^{2} \\ &=- 8 \pi + 4 \pi^{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(\pi,2)-f(2 \pi,0) \\ &=\left(- 8 \pi + 4 \pi^{2}\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=- 8 \pi + 4 \pi^{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(-1)&=\left\langle -3,-1\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r(1)&=\left\langle 3,1\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(-3,-1)&=- \left(-3\right)^{3} - \left(-1\right) \\ &=28, \\ f(3,1)&=- \left(3\right)^{3} - \left(1\right) \\ &=-28.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(3,1)-f(-3,-1) \\ &=\left(-28\right)-\left(28\right) \\ &=-56.
\end{aligned}
$$

The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(0)&=\left\langle 3,0\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)&=\left\langle 0,4\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(3,0)&=\left(3\right)^{2} \left(0\right) e^{\left(3\right) \left(0\right)} \\ &=0, \\ f(0,4)&=\left(0\right)^{2} \left(4\right) e^{\left(0\right) \left(4\right)} \\ &=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(0,4)-f(3,0) \\ &=\left(0\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

